```dart
class CounterView extends StatefulWidget {
  @override
  State<CounterView> createState() => _CounterViewState();
}

class _CounterViewState extends State<CounterView> {
  int _count = 0;

  void _increment() {
    setState(() {
      _count++;   // mutate, then tell Flutter to rebuild
    });
  }

  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Column(children: [
      Text('Count: $_count'),
      ElevatedButton(onPressed: _increment, child: Text('+')),
    ]);
  }
}
```

`setState` does two things: mutates `_count`, and tells Flutter this widget's subtree needs to rebuild. Mutating `_count` directly without calling `setState` would change the value but never trigger a rebuild — the UI would silently show stale data until something else happened to rebuild that subtree.

A common crash: an async call (a network request, a `Future.delayed`) started while a widget is on screen completes **after** that widget has been removed from the tree (navigated away, popped, conditionally removed) — and the completion handler calls `setState`, which throws, because there's no longer a valid `State` to update.

```dart
void _loadData() async {
  final data = await api.fetchData();
  if (!mounted) return;   // guard: check before touching state
  setState(() => _data = data);
}
```

The `mounted` check on `State` is the standard guard — it's `false` once `dispose()` has run. Skipping it is the Flutter equivalent of the delegate-not-weak or completion-handler-after-dismiss bugs on other platforms: the async work outlived the object that was supposed to receive its result.
